Width\":1.4,\"d\":\"m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75\"}]}]\n65:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"You can enable the extension by running the following \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"CREATE EXTENSION\"}],\" statement in the Neon \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"SQL Editor\"}],\" or from a client such as \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"psql\"}],\" that is connected to Neon. Creating a \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"partman\"}],\" schema is optional (but recommended) and you can name the schema whatever you like, but it cannot be changed after installation.\"]}]\n67:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"pg_partman\"}],\" extension does not require a superuser to run, but it's recommended to create a dedicated role for running \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"pg_partman\"}],\" functions and to act as the owner of all partition sets that \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"pg_partman\"}],\" will maintain.\"]}]\n68:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Here is a sample SQL script to create a dedicated role with the minimum required privileges, assuming that \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"pg_partman\"}],\" is installed to the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"partman\"}],\" schema and the dedicated role is named \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"partman_user\"}],\":\"]}]\n6a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"If the role needs to create schemas, you'll have to grant \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"CREATE\"}],\" on the database as well. This is only required if you give the role above the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"CREATE\"}],\" privilege on pre-existing schemas that will contain partition sets.\"]}]\n6c:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"When you create a new \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"Neon\"}],\" project, the default database name is \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"neondb\"}],\" and the default schema name is \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"public\"}],\". Replace \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"{WORKING_DATABASE_NAME}\"}],\" and \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"{WORKING_SCHEMA_NAME}\"}],\" with the actual database and schema names you want to manage the partitioned tables in. To find out more about the privileges needed to run \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"pg_partman\"}],\", refer to the \",[\"$\",\"$L63\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/pgpartman/pg_partman\",\"children\":\"pg_partman documentation\"}],\".\"]}]\n6d:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"For information about using the Neon SQL Editor, see \",[\"$\",\"$L63\",null,{\"href\":\"/docs/get-started/query-with-neon-sql-editor\",\"children\":\"Query with Neon's SQL Editor\"}],\". For information about using the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"psql\"}],\" client with Neon, see \",[\"$\",\"$L63\",null,{\"href\":\"/docs/connect/query-with-psql-editor\",\"children\":\"Connect with psql\"}],\".\"]}]\n6e:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Version Compatibility:\"}]}]\n6f:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"pg_partman\"}],\" works with Postgres 14 and above, complementing the native partitioning features introduced in these versions.\"]}]\n70:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"why-partition-your-data\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Why partition your data?\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#why-partition-your-data\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Why partition your data?\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$64:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n71:[\""])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"For tables that grow very large, partitioning offers several benefits:\"}]\n72:[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Faster queries:\"}],\" Partitioning allows Postgres to quickly locate and retrieve data within a specific partition, rather than scanning the entire table.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Scalability:\"}],\" Partitioning makes database administration simpler. For example, smaller partitions are easier to load and delete or back up and recover.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Managing the data lifecycle:\"}],\" Easier management of the data lifecycle by archiving or purging old partitions, which can be moved to cheaper storage options without affecting the active dataset.\"]}],\"\\n\"]}]\n73:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"native-partitioning-vs-pgpartman\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Native partitioning vs pg_partman\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#native-partitioning-vs-pgpartman\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Native partitioning vs pg_partman\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$64:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n74:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Postgres supports partitioning tables natively, with the following strategies to divide the data:\"}]\n75:[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"List partitioning\"}],\": Data is distributed across partitions based on a list of values, such as a category or location.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Range partitioning\"}],\": Data is distributed across partitions based on ranges of values, such as dates or numerical ranges.\"]}],\"\\n\"]}]\n76:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"With native partitioning, you need to manually create and manage partitions for your table.\"}]\n78:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"pg_partman\"}],\" supports creating partitions that are number or time-based, with each partition covering a range of values. It's designed for cases where partitions need to be created automatically as new records come in. So, list partitioning isn't applicable since the partition key values are not known in advance.\"]}]\n79:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"example-partitioning-user-activity-data\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Example: Partitioning user-activity data\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#example-partitioning-user-activity-data\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Example: Partitioning user-activity data\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$64:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n7a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Consider a social media platform that tracks user interactions in their website application, such as likes, comments, and shares. The data is stored in a table called \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"user_activities\"}],\", where \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"activity_type\"}],\" stores the type of activity and the other columns store additional information about the activity.\"]}]\n7b:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"setting-up-a-new-partitioned-table\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scro"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"ll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Setting up a new partitioned table\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#setting-up-a-new-partitioned-table\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Setting up a new partitioned table\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$64:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n7c:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Given the large volume of data generated by user interactions, partitioning the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"user_activities\"}],\" table can help keep queries manageable. Recent activity data is typically the most interesting for both the platform and its users, so \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"activity_time\"}],\" is a good candidate to partition on.\"]}]\n7d:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"We can create the partitioned table using the following 